<p><strong>Lost in Translation?!</strong></p>
<p></p>
<p>You’ve probably at some point come across a web site or publication that has been translated from another language into German so badly that its message ended up badly mangled, often so badly that you lost interest in the brand on offer and ended up finding what you were looking for elsewhere.</p>

<p>A native speaker is not enough</p>
<p>Talented translators are creative assets</p>
<p>Some basic mistakes</p>


<p>No matter how well a non-native English speaker thinks he or she can write English, it is not comparable to a translator who can write well, whose mother-tongue is English, who has an excellent knowledge of German, and who understands your business well.</p>

<p>All too common mistakes in texts that have been translated from German into English include, for example, German-influenced sentence structure, grammar, vocabulary and verb conjugations, not to mention the differences in style.</p>

<p>To give a couple of examples, how would you translate these into English?</p>
<ul>
<li><span title="“Please do not hesitate to contact us if you have any questions”; but certainly not “We are pleased for your questions”" style="color:blue">„Wir freuen uns auf Ihrer Fragen.“</span></li>
<li><span title="“Everything under one roof”, “Everything from a simgle source or “Your one-stop shop”; absolutely not “Everything from one hand” or “Everything out of one hand”" style="color:blue">„Alles aus einer Hand.“</span></li>
<li><span title="“Interested?”; NOT  “You are interested?“" style="color:blue">„Haben Sie Interesse?“</span></li>
<li><span title="“How to contact us”. “Thus you find us” is very wrong." style="color:blue">„So finden Sie uns.“</span></li>
<li><span title="Please call us for advice” is a lot more natural than “We advise you also gladly by Phone“" style="color:blue">„Wir beraten Sie auch gern telefonisch“.</span></li>
</ul>

<p>Sometimes, the translations of these simple phrases have an effect on the person reading it which is far from intended. Bad translations can, at the very least, produce a raised eyebrow or a snigger, and at the very worst, exasperate or provoke an outburst of laughter. Either way, your message is not taken seriously.

<p>We at Language Landscapes promise you – your message will not be lost in translation! It will read in English as if it were the original text and not a translation. It will be consistent and It will be delivered on time. And it will be of the highest quality.</p>
